muros (0.9.97) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Blocking bogon networks blocked seven well known ranges written into the
    source, not the several thousand prefixes that are unallocated today. The
    updater already downloaded the full list every night and wrote it next to
    the firewall, and nothing read it. The ruleset now builds its bogon sets
    from that list, and keeps the built-in ranges only until the first
    download. Private ranges marked as excluded in the list are left out, so
    blocking bogons no longer quietly blocks a private network the operator
    allowed.
  * The nightly download itself never ran: its action still called the
    FreeBSD helper that launches a background job, which does not exist here.
    Every other action had been ported, this one was missed, so a firewall
    installed months ago still carried the list of the day it was built.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:10:02 +0200

muros (0.9.96) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Four settings of the advanced firewall page had no effect at all.
    The maximum number of firewall states is applied to the connection
    tracking table, so a firewall sized for a large number of connections no
    longer keeps the kernel default and starts refusing connections with only
    a kernel message to show for it. The maximum number of entries per alias
    bounds the sets, so a downloaded list that grows without limit no longer
    takes the memory it wants. Traffic claiming port zero is dropped, as it
    always should have been, and the setting that turns that off works.
  * Packets dropped because they came from a private or unroutable source
    vanished without a trace, and the two settings meant to control that
    logging did nothing. They are logged, unless the operator says otherwise.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:03:44 +0200

muros (0.9.95) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The anti-lockout rule accepted ports 22, 80 and 443 on every interface,
    including the one facing the internet, ahead of every rule the operator
    wrote. On a firewall with a default deny policy and no rule at all, ssh
    and the web interface answered from the outside. An explicit block for
    those ports could not take effect either, since the rule came first. It is
    now bound to the primary inside interface.
  * The rule also carried a fixed list of ports, so an operator who had moved
    the web interface or ssh elsewhere was left with the wrong ports open on
    every interface and no protection at all on the right ones. It now carries
    the ports actually configured, and the redirection port only when the
    redirection is on.
  * The setting that turns the anti-lockout rule off was ignored. It is
    honoured.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:55:45 +0200

muros (0.9.94) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The address of the peer to replicate connection states to was read by the
    interface and ignored: the two nodes always looked for each other on a
    multicast group. On a sync link that is routed, or behind a switch that
    prunes multicast, they replicated into the void, which nothing reports and
    which is only discovered at the failover that drops every connection. When
    the peer is named, the states are now sent to it directly, in either
    address family.
  * Dropping the connections pinned to a gateway matched the mark exactly,
    which the flags the intrusion detection writes in the same mark could
    defeat. It matches the gateway number only.
  * Removed a setting the state replication daemon has deprecated and warned
    about on every start.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:42:27 +0200

muros (0.9.93) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Inline intrusion detection and policy based routing were writing in the
    same bits of the packet mark. A gateway is identified by its number, and
    the handover to suricata recognised an already inspected packet by the
    lowest bit of the mark, so any gateway with an odd number made the traffic
    routed through it skip inspection entirely, and any packet that came back
    from the inspection queue carried a mark the routing rules no longer
    recognised and left through the default gateway. The two flags of the
    intrusion detection now live above the sixteen bits the gateway numbers
    use, and the routing rules match their own bits only.
  * The captive portal froze the last activity of a client whose counters had
    been reset under it, which an idle timeout would eventually read as an
    idle client and disconnect.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:33:31 +0200

muros (0.9.92) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The captive portal redirected every encrypted connection of an
    unauthenticated client to itself, whatever its destination. The client got
    a certificate warning for a site it never asked the firewall about, and
    the portal stayed hidden behind it. Only the connections addressed to the
    firewall are redirected now, which is what a client detecting a captive
    network does.
  * The portal also intercepted plain web traffic between two clients of the
    same zone. Traffic aimed at an authenticated client is left alone.
  * The per-zone setting that hands the rules over to the operator was read by
    the interface and ignored by the enforcement, which built its redirection
    anyway. The zone is now left untouched.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:22:35 +0200

muros (0.9.91) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The rules the plugins register for themselves were shown on the rule page
    as automatically generated and never reached the kernel. Those rules are
    not in the configuration file, each plugin declares them at build time,
    and the nftables generator only ever read the configuration. A DHCP server
    only answered if the operator had written the rule by hand, an IPsec
    tunnel only came up if IKE and ESP had been allowed by hand, and the
    captive portal could not resolve a name before authentication. They are
    now collected and rendered ahead of the rules the operator wrote, where
    the page says they are.
  * The rule check tells a rule left out for good, and why, from a rule
    waiting for its schedule to open. Both were reported as scheduled, so a
    port forward on a protocol with no counterpart was announced as waiting
    for a time window that did not exist.
  * The rule check also names the rules a part of the system asked for and
    that could not be built.

