muros (0.9.88) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Outbound NAT rules were translated from a fraction of what the page
    stores. The address family, the protocol, the source and destination
    ports, the translation pool and its options, static port and logging were
    all read by the interface and dropped by the generator, so a rule limited
    to one protocol translated everything, a rule pointing at a pool of
    addresses translated to the interface address instead, and an IPv6 rule
    produced an IPv6 prefix under an IPv4 match, which made the whole ruleset
    unloadable.
  * Port forwards and 1:1 NAT follow the address family too, so an IPv6
    redirection or an IPv6 static mapping now reaches the kernel.
  * An address the generator cannot resolve is no longer passed to nft as a
    host name, where it either got resolved behind our back or broke the load.
    The rule is left out and named by the rule check.
  * NAT options with no counterpart here, reflection, bitmask and source-hash
    pools, firewall tags, translating to more than one address, are reported
    rather than dropped in silence.

