muros (0.9.85) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The traffic shaper can shape what arrives on an interface, which is the
    setting most people reach for first: capping a download. A queueing
    discipline only works on what leaves a device, and the older system shaped
    both directions from one rule, so the inbound half of such a rule silently
    did nothing here. An intermediate device now carries it, with the same tree
    of pipes and queues as any other device.
  * Classification for that direction is done by a filter of the traffic control
    layer, since the redirection happens before the packet filter is consulted.
    It understands a protocol, an address or a prefix and ports; a rule asking
    for more, an alias, a negation, a packet length, is named by the shaper
    notes rather than half applied.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 00:25:00 +0200

muros (0.9.84) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A pipe of the traffic shaper gets the queue it was configured with. The page
    has always offered the scheduler, the controlled delay targets, explicit
    congestion notification and the flow counts; the port read none of them and
    gave every pipe the same default, so a pipe tuned against bufferbloat
    behaved like one left alone. All of it is passed to the kernel now, and a
    pipe that adds delay still gets a delay queue.
  * What the kernel cannot express is named instead of ignored: the schedulers
    with no counterpart, the dynamic pipe per source or destination, the hash
    bucket count, and shaping what arrives on an interface, which needs an
    intermediate device and is not done yet. Printed when the shaper is applied,
    available as configctl shaper notes, and shown by the health audit.
  * Changing the scheduler of a pipe used to leave the old one in place.

