muros (0.9.83) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The table offenders are added to is filled again. A rule carrying a per
    source limit can name a table the sources that go over it are added to,
    which another rule blocks wholesale; the generator read the field and did
    nothing with it, so the limit forgot the source immediately and the block
    rule written against that table matched an empty set forever. The table is a
    set updated from the rule that catches the offender, under the name of the
    alias, so the block rule already written against it is the one that fills
    up. An entry lasts an hour. A table that already contains networks cannot be
    used this way and is reported rather than silently ignored.
  * Flushing the per source counters does something. The action was wired to a
    command that does nothing, so the button was a lie.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:20:00 +0200

muros (0.9.82) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A rule that asks for no connection tracking gets none. The setting exists
    for the traffic whose replies do not look like replies, and tracking it is
    what breaks it; the generator ignored the field, so those rules were tracked
    like every other. Their packets are now excluded ahead of connection
    tracking. Permissive tracking is what Linux already does by default.
  * Options that cannot be translated are named instead of dropped in silence.
    Sequence number modulation, answering the handshake on behalf of the host,
    global connection and source limits, the overload table, the type of service
    match, the 802.1p priority, per rule timeouts and accepting IP options are
    all reported by the rule check, which gained a verdict for a rule that is
    loaded but does not do everything it says.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:55:00 +0200

muros (0.9.81) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The quick option of a rule means something again. The older packet filter
    evaluated every rule and let the last match decide unless a rule was marked
    quick, which decided on the spot; nftables stops at the first match, so the
    port had turned every rule into a quick one. A floating rule without quick,
    whose entire purpose is to be overridden by a later interface rule, was
    winning instead: a broad floating block written to be refined further down
    the list stopped everything it covered. The rules are now ordered so that a
    first match walk picks exactly the rule the original semantics would have
    picked.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 22:35:00 +0200

muros (0.9.80) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A firewall rule limited to a time window is limited again. The generator
    never read the schedule of a rule, so a scheduled rule was permanently
    active: a pass rule written to close at the end of its window never closed,
    while the interface kept showing the schedule next to it. Weekday sets,
    calendar days and hour ranges are all honoured, a rule outside its window is
    not loaded at all, and the periodic filter reload that already existed for
    schedules finally has something to change.
  * The rule check tells a rule waiting for its window to open from one that
    will never be applied.

