muros (0.9.79) trixie; urgency=medium

  * A firewall rule can now be checked against the kernel. Every silent failure
    found in this port had the same shape: an item accepted by the interface,
    stored, listed back, and never turned into a rule. The new check walks the
    configuration and looks for each item in the ruleset the kernel runs and in
    the one the current configuration produces, which separates a rule that is
    applied, one that waits for a filter reload, and one that will never be
    applied at all. A rule loaded for an item that no longer exists is reported
    as stale. It runs from the console with configctl filter verify and as part
    of the health audit of the firmware page.
  * Every rule generated from a configuration item now carries the identifier of
    that item. Only the rules of the newer pages did, which is why the per rule
    statistics of the interface only ever worked for those: a rule from an older
    page carried its description instead and no counter could be attached to it.

