muros (0.9.77) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Wireless is driven through nl80211 instead of the FreeBSD ifconfig that no
    longer exists. The channel list, the transmit power table and the card
    capabilities now come from iw, so the interface form offers the channels the
    regulatory domain actually allows and stops declaring access point and
    ad-hoc modes unsupported on cards that support them. The wireless status
    page shows the scan results and the associated peers again, with what Linux
    reports about a peer: signal, negotiated rates, traffic counters, idle and
    connected time. All of it goes through configd, since the web server runs
    unprivileged.
  * An open wireless network works. hostapd owns the beacon in every case, so an
    access point without encryption had nothing to bring it up, and a station
    joining an open network had no supplicant: both are started as soon as a
    network name is configured. The hidden network, quality of service and
    station isolation toggles reach hostapd, and the generated configuration
    files, which carry the passphrase, are written private.
  * hostapd, wpasupplicant and wireless-regdb are recommended packages.

