muros (0.9.75) trixie; urgency=medium

  * One-to-one NAT and IPv6 prefix translation exist again. Both moved from the
    old configuration section to the model tree, where the current GUI writes
    them, and the ruleset builder only ever read the old location: a mapping
    was configured, listed in the GUI, and never rendered. They are built now,
    and a mapping between whole prefixes uses the nftables netmap statement,
    which rewrites the host part inside the prefix the way pf did with binat,
    while single addresses keep the plain form. The external side of a
    one-to-one rule inherits the mask of its internal side, as pf derived it,
    and both sides are masked to their network so a rule shows what it really
    translates. NPTv6 is not checksum neutral the way RFC 6296 describes,
    nftables has no such rewrite, connection tracking fixes the checksums
    instead.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:05:00 +0200

muros (0.9.74) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Firewall rules limit their sources again. A pass rule can cap how many
    connections a single source holds and how fast it opens them, the options
    are in the configuration and the GUI still offers them under Advanced
    features, but the generated ruleset dropped them: a rule meant to slow a
    brute force down passed everything. Both become a dynamic set keyed by the
    source address, one per rule and per address family, and a drop rule placed
    just above the pass rule it guards. Only new connections are tested, the
    counting set is reference counted by connection tracking and the rate set
    ages its sources out. The pf state options that count globally, max and
    max-src-nodes, stay on the roadmap.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:55:00 +0200

muros (0.9.73) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Multi-WAN failover moves the connections that were already open. The
    ruleset and the routing tables followed the surviving uplink, but every
    established flow kept the mark of the gateway it was pinned to and stayed
    steered into its table, so a failover only helped new connections. This is
    what "pfctl -k gateway" did on FreeBSD, and connection tracking holds no
    gateway to kill on, so the equivalent is to drop the tracked connections
    carrying the mark of the gateway that changed state, and of the groups
    holding it. The routing reconfiguration does it right after reloading the
    ruleset, and the configd action that claimed the routing subsystem handled
    it, while doing nothing at all, now runs the same script.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 18:40:00 +0200

muros (0.9.72) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Multi-WAN failover: a rule pinned to a gateway group now leaves through
    that group. The ruleset already marked those flows exactly like a rule
    pinned to a single gateway, but only real gateways were given a routing
    table, so the mark landed on nothing and the traffic silently followed the
    main table. A group gets its table too, holding the members of the highest
    tier that still satisfies its trigger, as a multipath route weighted by the
    gateway configuration when that tier holds several uplinks. The failover
    falls out of it: the monitor reloads the ruleset when a member changes
    state, the tables are rebuilt from the live status, and the group starts
    using what is left.
  * Packaging: a box can tell which build it runs. The core version token had
    been left behind at 0.9.14 while the package moved on, and the commit and
    the hash stayed at their placeholder, so the firmware page reported a
    version that never existed. They are derived at build time now, the version
    from the package version and the commit from the checkout.

