muros (0.9.71) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Host discovery: the service exists on Debian. OPNsense shipped hostwatch as
    a FreeBSD binary reading ARP, NDP and DHCP traffic from a bpf descriptor,
    and neither that binary nor its rc script were carried over, so configd
    could not even start the service and the host list, the captive portal, the
    MAC aliases and the Kea prefix watcher only ever saw the neighbour table.
    The daemon is written for Linux now: a packet socket per interface with a
    filter on ARP and the ICMPv6 neighbour discovery messages, promiscuous when
    asked, completed by the kernel neighbour table for the hosts learned before
    it started. It writes the same database and the same view, keeps only the
    addresses that belong to a prefix of the interface they were seen on, and
    is probed on its pid file. Its settings move from the rc.conf.d fragment to
    a generated hostwatch.conf, and the setup script stops handing the
    directories to an account Debian does not have.
  * Power savings: the setting does something again. It ran powerd(8), a
    FreeBSD daemon, so nothing happened at all. The four modes are translated
    into the closest governor the cpufreq driver of the machine offers, the
    energy performance preference is set where the platform exposes it, and the
    AC line is followed to switch between the battery and the mains mode.
  * High availability: a DHCP failover peer gets its interface again. The
    synchronisation guessed it from the output of netstat and route, two
    FreeBSD tools, and always came up empty, which dropped the failover
    configuration on the way to the backup. The kernel is asked directly with
    a route lookup.
  * DHCP: the delegated prefixes are routed to their client. The watcher
    installed those routes with route(8), which does not exist here, so a
    prefix delegation was handed out and never reachable. iproute2 installs it,
    and moves it to the new client in the same call.
  * Monitoring: monit starts. The port kept the FreeBSD path of the binary, and
    the configuration test read the control file of the Debian package instead
    of the generated one. Both point at what MurOS renders now, the include
    directory is created before the daemon reads it, the package is recommended
    and the unit it ships is kept from running a second instance.
  * Diagnostics: the netstat tabs that reported the FreeBSD kernel are filled
    with what Linux accounts. Netisr becomes the per CPU softirq receive
    statistics, bpf becomes the packet sockets with the process holding them,
    and the mbuf zone usage becomes the socket memory charged against the
    pressure limits of the kernel next to the skbuff caches, which is also what
    the dashboard gauge draws.
  * System: the sensor inventory comes from the temperature collector, the
    sysctl call it used to run is FreeBSD only. The PPP interfaces are no
    longer renamed through netgraph, they are pppd devices since the port, and
    the pool maintenance no longer calls daemon(8).

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 17:45:00 +0200

muros (0.9.70) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Boot: the services the configuration asks for are started again. FreeBSD
    started them from the rc.conf.d fragments, one enable flag per service, and
    rc.freebsd is gone: the DHCP server, the captive portal, the intrusion
    detection, the monitoring, the host discovery, the netflow exporter and the
    traffic shaper all stayed down after a reboot until an operator applied their
    page. The last step of the boot chain now starts what the plugins declare and
    that is not running yet.
  * DHCP: Kea starts on the configuration generated from the model. The hook that
    writes /etc/kea was only called by the rc script, so the daemons came up on
    whatever was in that directory, the stock Debian sample on a fresh install.
    The wrapper runs the hook itself, and the keactrl template loses the FreeBSD
    paths it described for a tool that no longer runs the daemons.
  * Intrusion detection: suricata can be started at all. The configd actions
    pointed at an rc script the port never carried over, and the capture modes were
    FreeBSD only, a pcap live mode and netmap or an ipfw divert socket. Detection
    listens with af-packet on the selected interfaces, enforcement reads the
    netfilter queue and the generated ruleset hands the packets over in chains
    evaluated before the firewall chains. Repeat mode marks what suricata lets
    through so it is not queued twice, and the bypass flag keeps the traffic
    flowing when the service is stopped. A configuration imported from OPNsense is
    read as af-packet for pcap and as the queue for netmap and divert.
  * Intrusion detection: rotation of the alert and stats files moves from
    newsyslog to logrotate, with a USR2 to make suricata reopen them.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 10:30:00 +0200

muros (0.9.69) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Firewall: a rule on an interface group matches that group again. The
    generator only knew the interfaces of config.xml, so a group name resolved to
    nothing and the rule kept no interface match at all, which means it applied to
    every interface: a block on one group became a block on everything and a pass
    became a hole through the firewall. Groups are now expanded to the devices of
    their members, for rules, outbound NAT, port forwards and one to one NAT.
  * Interfaces: group membership is kept as state instead of ifconfig group
    attachments, and reported in the interface details.
  * Firewall: the pf ruleset body that filter_configure_sync() stopped executing
    when the nftables generator took over is gone, five hundred lines of scrub,
    aliases, legacy rules, pfctl loading and pf table sizing, with the helpers
    only it used.
  * Importer: the configuration importer works at all. It loaded ZFS with kldstat
    and kldload, listed disks with camcontrol, gmirror and graid, and probed
    partitions by the FreeBSD names s1a, p3, s1 and p1, so on Debian the device
    list was empty and no partition ever matched. Disks come from lsblk, types
    from blkid, and every partition carrying a readable file system is tried until
    one holds an installation, which also handles a separate boot or EFI
    partition. The password reset chroot bind mounts dev, proc and sys.
  * System: the RAM disk options for /var/log and /tmp and the swap file option
    do something again. They were applied at boot by rc scripts nothing calls,
    built on mtree, hw.physmem and swapinfo; they are systemd mount and swap units
    now, written from the configuration by the page and by the boot chain. The RAM
    disks apply on the next reboot, as they did on FreeBSD.
  * Boot: the last FreeBSD rc leftovers are removed, the crash dump helper built
    on dumpon and savecore, the live media mode, and the installer wrapper around
    bsdinstall that the Debian live installer of the image replaces.

