muros (0.9.68) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Web interface: the administration page reconfigures the front end that is
    actually serving. The package ships a lighttpd configuration and a unit
    drop-in pointing at it, but webgui_configure_do() generated a different
    configuration elsewhere, killed a pid file systemd does not own and spawned a
    second lighttpd whose PHP backend was php-cgi at a FreeBSD path. Changing the
    port, the certificate, the ciphers, HSTS or the listening interfaces had no
    effect. It now generates where the unit reads, restarts php-fpm and lighttpd
    through systemd, serves PHP from the packaged pool and reports the state of
    the unit. Three directives fixed on the way: index-file.names, an empty
    element in url.access-deny, and the server tag.
  * Web interface: the PHP settings that come from the configuration, the
    timezone, the session timeout, the output compression and the error reporting,
    are written as drop-ins for the fpm and cli SAPIs instead of a php.ini in the
    FreeBSD locations where nothing reads it. The timezone in particular never
    left the packaged default.
  * Captive portal: the control script was still an rc.subr script, so every
    start, stop and restart died on the missing /etc/rc.subr. It is a plain
    script now, the zone chroots get device nodes instead of a devfs mount, the
    workers and the API dispatcher run as www-data, and the dispatcher hands PHP
    to the php-fpm pool instead of php-cgi, without which every portal
    authentication failed.
  * configd: the credential of the caller is read with SO_PEERCRED. The FreeBSD
    xucred path left the session context empty on Debian, so every action with an
    allowed_groups constraint was refused, the five authentication actions
    included. wheel and wwwonly become root and www-data, here, in the capture
    download and in the test configuration.
  * Logging: the messages come from the journal. The pipeline still read
    /dev/klog and the FreeBSD datagram sockets, so syslog-ng saw nothing but its
    own internal messages and every log page was empty. The configuration version
    token, substituted with nothing, now says 4.8. The newsyslog template is gone,
    log_archive does the rotation. The package log matches apt and dpkg.
  * Logging: the lockout handler blocks again, with an nftables set element and a
    conntrack flush of the address as source and as destination, instead of pfctl
    and pfctl -k.
  * Firewall: no more filterlog daemon spawned on pflog0 at every reload, and no
    more bogonsv6 flush through pfctl. The firewall log is the kernel messages
    carrying the ruleset marker, which is what the filter log and its remote
    destinations select now.

 -- MurOS <jpzi@proton.me>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 09:55:00 +0200

muros (0.9.67) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Boot: the boot lock and the syshook levels are back. Removing /etc/rc left
    nobody holding the exclusive lock on /var/run/booting, so product::booting()
    was always false: exit_on_bootup() deferred nothing during the boot chain and
    the boot performance log stayed empty. Nothing ran the early and start syshook
    levels either, so the templates were not regenerated before the chain and the
    deferred work of the start level (dynamic addresses acquired during the chain,
    syslog and cron restarts, tunables of late loaded modules) never happened. The
    boot unit now runs the PHP subr, the early level, the chain under flock, the
    start level, and the stop level on shutdown, which until now only an operator
    reboot from the interface triggered.
  * Boot: the early syshooks are Debian. 05-upgrade installed the kernel, base and
    package sets staged by opnsense-update, which does not exist here; apt applies
    everything before the reboot, so the hook finishes an interrupted dpkg run
    instead. 10-configd starts the unit rather than the rc script. The rc scripts
    of configd and flowd_aggregate are gone with them.
  * Boot: the DHCP lease watcher is ordered after the boot chain. Its path and
    timer were pulled in by paths.target and timers.target, long before the
    interfaces are configured, and networkd usually gets its first lease exactly
    there: the handler then replayed the newwanip chain and asked configd for a
    reconfiguration while the chain was still configuring the same interface. The
    handler also checks the boot lock itself for the window the ordering cannot
    cover.
  * System: the boot and shutdown melodies play again. opnsense-beep tested
    /dev/speaker and ran the FreeBSD beep port with a pitch in units of 1/100 s;
    it now uses beep(1), which drives the PC speaker on Linux and takes hertz and
    milliseconds. The melody files keep their units. The binary is a suggestion,
    a machine without a PC speaker exits silently.
  * Templates: the generated /etc/rc.conf.d fragments of the ported services are
    gone, they were read by rc.freebsd. The netflow template keeps netflow.conf,
    the shaper template disappears entirely since tc_apply.py reads the model,
    and so do the dead shaper scripts (dummynet statistics and its library, the
    ipfw table updater, the ipfw and pf load order fixup, the dnctl setup).

