muros (0.9.59) trixie; urgency=medium

  * PPPoE: sessions are run by pppd with the rp-pppoe plugin under a
    muros-pppoe@<interface>.service template, instead of an mpd5 configuration
    driven through a netgraph control socket. None of that exists on Debian, so
    a PPPoE WAN never came up at all. The generated options file carries the
    service name, host-uniq, MTU and MRU, the compression switches, the idle
    timeout and demand dialling, the peer nameservers when they may be used and
    IPv6CP when the interface asks for it. pppd names the session interface
    itself, so the device recorded in the configuration is unchanged. The other
    PPP types now log an explicit unsupported message.
  * PPPoE: the link scripts are reached through the pppd hook directories
    (/etc/ppp/{ip,ipv6}-{up,down}.d), which nothing installed, and the down
    script no longer shuts down a netgraph node nor stamps the session log with
    date -j. The uptime helper used the BSD stat and date spellings and printed
    nothing.
  * Wireless: ported to Debian. The interface type is set with iw on the
    wireless device itself (managed, access point, ibss) instead of expecting an
    ifconfig wlan clone, hostapd is generated for nl80211 with the band, the
    channel and the regulatory country that ifconfig used to apply, and both
    daemons are started from their Debian paths. Regulatory domain, transmit
    power and the channel outside access point mode are applied with iw.
    Diversity, antenna selection, turbo, protection mode, apbridge and WEP have
    no Linux equivalent and are reported as ignored.
  * Dependencies: ethtool, iw and ppp.

