    The TOTP enrolment URI advertised issuer "OPNsense" and an icon hosted on
    docs.opnsense.org; it now uses issuer "MurOS" and the MurOS icon, so
    authenticator apps show the right name and logo.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sat, 27 Jun 2026 16:20:00 +0200

muros (0.9.49) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Firmware: make the status and health pages load fast again. The health
    audit verified file integrity by checksumming every file of every installed
    package (debsums -s, or dpkg --verify with no argument as a fallback), which
    walked the whole filesystem and took about 60 seconds. It now scopes the
    integrity scan to the appliance's own footprint (the core meta package, the
    os-* plugins and the kernel images), matching the original intent of only
    verifying the base and kernel sets. Health drops from ~60s to ~5s.
  * Firmware: the status check ran apt's dependency solver twice per refresh,
    once to list pending packages and once to total the download size. A single
    --print-uris dist-upgrade pass now yields both, cutting the synchronous
    status check from ~6.3s to ~3.6s.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sat, 27 Jun 2026 16:05:00 +0200

muros (0.9.48) trixie; urgency=medium

  * IPsec: make route-based (VTI) tunnels actually carry traffic on Linux. The
    swanctl child of a tunnel bound to a VTI device now emits if_id_in and
    if_id_out equal to the VTI reqid, which is the id of the ipsec<reqid> xfrm
    interface created for it. On FreeBSD the reqid alone bound the if_ipsec
    device, but the Linux XFRM stack only forwards a child's traffic through the
    xfrm interface when the negotiated SAs are tagged with its if_id. Both the
    connections model (Swanctl) and the legacy phase1/phase2 generator set this,
    and only for children whose reqid matches a configured VTI so plain tunnels
    that pin a reqid keep their normal behaviour.

 -- MurOS <dev@muros.org>  Sat, 27 Jun 2026 15:45:00 +0200

muros (0.9.47) trixie; urgency=medium

  * High availability: port the CARP/pfsync data plane to native Debian tooling.
    CARP virtual IPs (virtualip/vip mode=carp) are now realised as keepalived
    VRRP instances and the pfsync state channel as conntrackd. A new generator
    (vrrp.inc) renders /etc/keepalived/keepalived.conf and
    /etc/conntrackd/conntrackd.conf from config.xml and starts the services only
    when CARP VIPs and an HA sync interface are configured. The advskew is
    mapped to the VRRP priority, addresses sharing an interface and vhid are
    grouped into one instance, and a notify hook records the master/backup role
    and drives the conntrackd failover. The CARP status, maintenance and
    enable/disable controls and the monit CARP check now read this state instead
    of the FreeBSD net.inet.carp.* sysctls. keepalived and conntrackd move to
    Recommends and are disabled at boot (MurOS starts them on demand).

